	<div class="notes"><p>Édouard Baldus<br /><em>Arles, Cloître St. Trophime</em><br />c. 1861 · The Metropolitan Museum of Art</p><details><summary>Light transport, not a glow sticker</summary><p>The same forward Snell-ray model as <a href="/lab/interfaces/caustics">Borrowed sunlight</a> projects equal incident flux through eight prescribed water-wave modes. Actual refraction-map compression produces brighter regions; sparse arrivals produce darker ones. Moving a bounded surface bump changes where the rays land. There is no autonomous animation.</p><p>The 3803 × 3162 original and all twelve mip levels are immutable. A fixed crop samples that native source in linear light. Received irradiance modulates its exposure with ambient fill and a bounded photographic shoulder; unit illumination reproduces the source. The final pass encodes sRGB. This treats the print as a flat receiver, not recovered 3D architecture or calibrated photographic reflectance. It cannot infer shadows or occlusion from the pictured scene.</p><p>The optical flux attachment remains independent of the photograph and exposure. A finite receiver has incoming and outgoing boundary flux; local regions need not preserve their previous total light. Native output and light attachments follow fractional CSS size × actual DPR, including 3×. Source/mips add 64,123,008 bytes before native output, ray storage and light attachments. Original and radial comparisons unmount the GPU model entirely.</p><p><a href="https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/283136" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Original photograph · public domain ↗</a><br /><a href="https://people.compute.dtu.dk/jerf/papers/diffsplat_lowres.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Photon Differential Splatting, 2014 ↗</a><br /><a href="https://pbr-book.org/4ed/Textures_and_Materials/Image_Texture" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Image footprints and linear-light filtering ↗</a></p></details></div>
